This is Circadia, an astronomical watch face I just released [on Connect IQ.]

\textbf{How it works:} The clip above is a full 24-hour cycle. The sun and moon move in real time based on your location. The 12 o'clock position is your northern horizon, with the centre directly overhead. Face north, and the sky on your watch matches the sky above you.  

As the sun sets, its halo shifts from yellow to red, reaching purple at nautical twilight, then blue by astronomical twilight. Full night begins once the halo is completely below the horizon. Stars fade in through nautical twilight, reaching peak brightness by full night. At sunrise, the transition reverses. The minute digits take on the halo's colour, while the hour digits briefly flare orange through sunset and sunrise. 

The moon shows its current phase and position, with the illuminated part always facing the sun - just as it does in the real sky.

\textbf{What makes it different:} Most astronomical faces feel a bit cluttered or cartoonish to me. I wanted something mathematically detailed but visually quiet. Just a clean, minimal watch face, with the sky drifting by underneath a simple clock and a few user-specified data fields. It's a "normal" watch face first, but one that reminds you that the sun is setting, or that the moon is full. 

\textbf{Caveats:} This is my first watch face. I've tested it on FR265S and Vivoactive 5, but there are probably weird bugs I haven't hit. Feedback is very welcome, including suggestions for additional options and data fields. Currently released for AMOLED devices, with MIP support planned once I can test it.

